In C, a program terminates when it reaches the end of its main function. Before the main function ends, the program can call the exit function to terminate the program. The exit function takes a single argument, which is the exit status. The exit status is a non-negative integer that is returned to the operating system when the program terminates. The exit status can be used by the operating system to determine how the program ended. The exit status can be 0 to indicate success, or a non-zero value to indicate failure. Here is an example of using the exit function:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main ( void )
{
        int x = 0;
        if ( x == 0 ) {
                printf ( "<s>Error</s>" ) ;
                exit ( 1 ) ;
        }
        printf ( "<s>Success</s>" ) ;
}
```

In this example, the program checks the value of x. If x is 0, the program prints an error message and terminates with an exit status of 1. If x is not 0, the program prints a success message. The program always terminates with an exit status of 0 or 1.
